['customers', 'conway_gen_zero', 'web_devices', 'web_demographics', 'channels_dim', 'gender_dim', 'packaging', 'packaging_relations', 'product_groups', 'products', 'monthly_sales', 'breweries', 'purchases', 'product_alcohol', 'customer_favorites', 'customer_reviews', 'locations', 'inventory', 'orders', 'orderlines', 'monthly_budget', 'product_minimums', 'stock', 'ticker', 'web_apps', 'web_pages', 'web_counter_hist', 'server_heartbeat', 'web_page_visits', 'employees', 'emp_hire_periods', 'picking_list', 'picking_line', 'picking_log', 'id_name_type', 'id_name_coll_type', 'id_name_coll_entries', 'favorite_coll_type']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
orders.id : filters just order 423  
orders.customer_id / ordered : context, not strictly necessary for picking  

orderlines.order_id : join to orders (423)  
orderlines.product_id : which products have to be picked  
orderlines.qty        : how many of each product are required (may occur several times)  
orderlines.id         : keeps the original line sequence (important when an order contains several lines for the same product)

inventory.product_id  : join to orderlines to see what is on‑hand  
inventory.qty         : quantity available in that specific location (what can be picked)  
inventory.location_id : where the stock physically lies  
inventory.purchase_id : allows us to look up the purchase date – an earlier purchase is picked first  

purchases.id          : join to inventory.purchase_id  
purchases.purchased   : the actual purchase date, used to prioritise “earlier first”  

locations.id          : join to inventory.location_id  
locations.warehouse   : we only want rows where warehouse = 1  
locations.aisle       : to show which aisle the picker must walk to  
locations.position    : exact shelf / bin position  

Typical conditions that will be used  
WHERE orderlines.order_id = 423  
AND   locations.warehouse = 1  
JOIN  inventory        ON inventory.product_id = orderlines.product_id  
JOIN  locations        ON locations.id        = inventory.location_id  
LEFT  JOIN purchases   ON purchases.id        = inventory.purchase_id  

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Below are ten exploratory queries (each different) that gradually collect all the information needed to calculate the pick quantities.  
All queries are capped with LIMIT 20 to stay small.

```sql
-- 1) See what lines exist on order 423
SELECT DISTINCT id AS orderline_id,
       product_id,
       qty
FROM orderlines
WHERE order_id = 423
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) What inventory do we have in warehouse 1?
SELECT DISTINCT i.id          AS inventory_id,
       i.product_id,
       i.qty,
       i.location_id
FROM inventory AS i
JOIN locations  AS l ON l.id = i.location_id
WHERE l.warehouse = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Join orderlines and warehouse‑1 inventory, product by product
SELECT DISTINCT ol.id         AS orderline_id,
       ol.product_id,
       ol.qty           AS order_qty,
       i.id             AS inventory_id,
       i.qty            AS stock_qty
FROM orderlines AS ol
JOIN inventory  AS i  ON i.product_id = ol.product_id
JOIN locations  AS l  ON l.id = i.location_id
WHERE ol.order_id   = 423
  AND l.warehouse   = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Add purchase dates so we can later sort “earliest first”
SELECT DISTINCT ol.id           AS orderline_id,
       ol.product_id,
       i.id             AS inventory_id,
       i.qty            AS stock_qty,
       p.purchased
FROM orderlines AS ol
JOIN inventory  AS i  ON i.product_id   = ol.product_id
LEFT JOIN purchases AS p ON p.id        = i.purchase_id
JOIN locations  AS l  ON l.id           = i.location_id
WHERE ol.order_id = 423
  AND l.warehouse = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Full location details (aisle & position) for each matching inventory row
SELECT DISTINCT ol.id           AS orderline_id,
       ol.product_id,
       i.qty            AS stock_qty,
       p.purchased,
       l.aisle,
       l.position
FROM orderlines AS ol
JOIN inventory  AS i  ON i.product_id = ol.product_id
LEFT JOIN purchases AS p ON p.id      = i.purchase_id
JOIN locations  AS l  ON l.id         = i.location_id
WHERE ol.order_id = 423
  AND l.warehouse = 1
ORDER BY ol.id, p.purchased, i.qty     -- earliest & smallest first
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Aggregate how much stock we have per product in warehouse 1
SELECT ol.product_id,
       SUM(i.qty) AS total_stock
FROM orderlines AS ol
JOIN inventory  AS i  ON i.product_id = ol.product_id
JOIN locations  AS l  ON l.id         = i.location_id
WHERE ol.order_id = 423
  AND l.warehouse = 1
GROUP BY ol.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Compare order quantity vs. total stock (sanity‑check no shortages)
SELECT ol.product_id,
       SUM(ol.qty)               AS ordered_qty,
       SUM(i.qty)                AS stock_qty
FROM orderlines AS ol
JOIN inventory  AS i  ON i.product_id = ol.product_id
JOIN locations  AS l  ON l.id         = i.location_id
WHERE ol.order_id = 423
  AND l.warehouse = 1
GROUP BY ol.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Show inventory rows per product sorted by “oldest purchase then smallest qty”
SELECT i.product_id,
       i.id           AS inventory_id,
       p.purchased,
       i.qty,
       l.aisle,
       l.position
FROM inventory AS i
LEFT JOIN purchases AS p ON p.id = i.purchase_id
JOIN locations  AS l ON l.id = i.location_id
WHERE l.warehouse = 1
  AND i.product_id IN (SELECT product_id FROM orderlines WHERE order_id = 423)
ORDER BY i.product_id, p.purchased, i.qty
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Bring in the order‑line sequence so we can pick in the exact same order
SELECT ol.id         AS orderline_id,
       ol.product_id,
       ol.qty        AS needed,
       i.id          AS inventory_id,
       i.qty         AS in_location,
       p.purchased,
       l.aisle,
       l.position
FROM orderlines AS ol
JOIN inventory  AS i  ON i.product_id = ol.product_id
LEFT JOIN purchases AS p ON p.id      = i.purchase_id
JOIN locations  AS l  ON l.id         = i.location_id
WHERE ol.order_id = 423
  AND l.warehouse = 1
ORDER BY ol.id, p.purchased, i.qty
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) (Final “ready to feed the algorithm” view)
-- The view is ordered so that a cursor can walk through each product
-- and subtract quantities until the cumulative need is satisfied.
SELECT ol.id             AS orderline_id,
       ol.product_id,
       ol.qty            AS line_need,
       i.id              AS inventory_id,
       i.qty             AS bin_qty,
       p.purchased,
       l.aisle,
       l.position
FROM orderlines AS ol
JOIN inventory  AS i  ON i.product_id = ol.product_id
LEFT JOIN purchases AS p ON p.id      = i.purchase_id
JOIN locations  AS l  ON l.id         = i.location_id
WHERE ol.order_id = 423
  AND l.warehouse = 1
ORDER BY ol.product_id, p.purchased, i.qty           -- earliest & smallest first
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries expose every piece of information you need in order to calculate, step‑by‑step, exactly how many units to pick from each location for order 423 while obeying the rules:  

• restrict to warehouse 1,  
• never pick more than is in a bin,  
• pick the oldest purchases first,  
• when quantities tie, empty the smaller bin first,  
• respect the cumulative quantities across multiple order lines of the same product.
